  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "Commute times are estimated using travel times between subdistrict centroids rather than precise individual addresses.",
      "what": "The study analyzed morning peak-hour commuting times for workers across 290 Chinese cities by linking the 2015 China 1% Population Sample Survey with Baidu Maps API route queries. It modeled driving, walking, cycling, and public transit travel times between residential and workplace subdistrict centroids during the 7:00–9:00 weekday window. The analysis revealed that workers holding local hukou status have estimated one-way commutes that are 11.8 minutes shorter than non-local workers, with this institutional gap being narrower in denser, more spatially compact cities.",
      "why": "It establishes that institutional hukou status is a stronger determinant of commuting inequality in urban China than education or occupation, and demonstrates that compact urban form and population concentration can actively mitigate these institutional disparities."
    }
